'''/toju''' is a highly secretive [[Balisca|Baliscan]] {{W|anonymity|anonymous}}, {{W|autonomism|autonomous}} {{W|Black hat (computer security)|black hat}} {{W|hacktivism|hacktivist}} {{W|collective}} and movement primarily known for its various cyberattacks against several governments, government institutions and government agencies, politicians, corporations and the {{W|Catholic Church}}. The group takes its name from the [[Maroniko language]], and is typically translated as "protection" or "hideout", and the {{W|forward slash}} is used to imitate the formatting of a chat command (e.g., writing "/toju" as though there were such a command). /toju does not appear to hack for financial profit, claiming their main motivation is to "have fun by causing problems for bad people". Their work is distinguished by their focus on the possible comedic and entertainment value of attacking targets. In their hacks the group shows affiliation with the autonomous ''Hackbloc'' collective.

In its early form, the concept was adopted by a decentralized {{W|online community}} acting anonymously in a coordinated manner, usually toward a loosely self-agreed goal and primarily focused on entertainment. The earliest members were part of the first {{W|generation}} in the country to have received government-backed {{W|computer science education}}—and thus received enthusiastic support in their pioneering hacking.  The group became increasingly associated with collaborative hacktivism on a number of issues internationally, especially those involving exposing corruption. Individuals claiming to align themselves with /toju undertook {{W|DoS}} attacks and other actions in retaliation against copyright-focused campaigns by motion picture and recording industry trade associations. Later targets of /toju hacktivism included government agencies of {{W|Israel}}, {{W|Saudi Arabia}}, {{W|Iran}}, [[Argentina]] and others; the {{W|Islamic State}}; {{W|Online hate speech|online hate sites}}; {{W|copyright protection}} agencies; the [[W:Westboro Baptist Church|Westboro Baptist Church]]; and corporations such as {{W|BP}}, {{W|Shell}}, {{W|Deutsche Bank}}, [[W:Alphabet Inc.|Alphabet]] and [[W:Amazon (company)|Amazon]]. /toju has not aligned itself with any other entities, but has endorsed the activities of {{W|WikiLeaks}}, supported the {{W|Occupy movement}}, and has supported {{W|Chelsea Manning}}, {{W|Edward Snowden}} and the practice of [[W:Whistleblowing|whistleblowing]]. Members of /toju have carried out cyberattacks on confederal agencies, media, companies, military contractors, military personnel, and police officers, resulting in increased attention of Baliscan intelligence services toward the groups' activities.

Evaluations of the group's actions and effectiveness vary widely. Supporters have called the group "{{W|freedom fighters}}", digital {{W|Robin Hood}}s, while international critics have described them as "{{W|social justice warriors}}", "a cyber {{W|woke}} lynch-mob" or "cyber terrorists". Interest in /toju experienced a significant revival during the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]], during which it's members took down or disrupted various portions of Balisca's {{W|Government by algorithm|algocratic government}} {{W|social machine}} and interfered with the [[Mozos de Escuadra]]'s operations attempting to dismantle emerging [[autonomous commune (Balisca)|autonomous communes]].

== Philosophy ==
In general, the /toju advocates {{W|radical transparency}} in government, {{W|freedom of information}}, and the {{W|human right}} to communication. Broadly speaking, /toju opposes {{W|internet censorship}} and control and the majority of their actions target governments, organizations, and corporations that they accuse of censorship. Supporting the principles of the {{W|hacker ethic}}, the group also fights for free universal access to computers and technological infrastructure as well as the use of {{W|free open-source software}}. /toju spreads an entrepreneurial vision refusing {{W|capitalism|capitalist}} control.

